CAT = "pltQuestioning"; SEG = "QUE"
Q = [
("After asking a question, a teacher waits several seconds before calling on anyone. The most likely result is:",
 "Longer, more thoughtful answers", ["Fewer students answering", "More off-task behavior", "Faster pace with less depth"],
 "Increasing wait time to three seconds or more is linked to longer answers, more students responding and higher-level thinking."),
("During a lesson on magnets, which question best promotes higher-level thinking?",
 "\"Why do you think the nail stuck?\"", ["\"What color is this magnet?\"", "\"Is this magnet heavy?\"", "\"What is this object called?\""],
 "Asking why invites students to explain and reason about cause. The others ask for simple observation or recall."),
("A student answers a question incorrectly and several classmates laugh. What should the teacher do first?",
 "Restate the class norm of respect", ["Ignore the laughter and go on", "Ask the student to sit out", "Laugh along to ease the moment"],
 "A non-critical classroom depends on norms that protect students who take risks. Addressing the laughter at once keeps answering safe for everyone."),
("A teacher asks, \"What do you already know about volcanoes?\" before a science unit. The main purpose of this question is to:",
 "Determine prior knowledge", ["Assign a unit grade", "Summarize the whole unit", "Bring the lesson to an end"],
 "Opening questions that ask what students know help the teacher find starting points and misconceptions."),
("A student says, \"It's like... the water goes up, um, into the air?\" The teacher replies, \"So you're saying the water turns into a gas and rises?\" This is:",
 "Restatement", ["Redirection", "Wait time", "Reprimand"],
 "Restating or paraphrasing a student's idea in clearer words helps the student articulate the thought and confirms understanding."),
("Which practice best encourages all students, not only volunteers, to participate in questioning?",
 "Random calls after think time", ["Calling only on raised hands", "Letting the fastest answer first", "Asking only yes-or-no questions"],
 "Calling on students randomly after think time signals that everyone is expected to think, not just the few who volunteer."),
("During a class discussion about a book, students disagree strongly. Which rule best keeps the discussion safe and open?",
 "Respond to ideas, not to people", ["Only the teacher may disagree", "Stop when anyone disagrees", "Vote to decide the right answer"],
 "Norms such as challenging ideas rather than classmates let students take risks and respect diverse opinions."),
("A teacher nods, leans forward and keeps eye contact while a shy student speaks. This shows the use of:",
 "Nonverbal communication", ["Verbal prompting", "Written feedback", "Formal assessment"],
 "Body language, eye contact and facial expression communicate interest and encouragement without words."),
("A teacher learns that in some students' cultures, looking an adult directly in the eye is a sign of disrespect. The best response is to:",
 "Not treat it as defiance", ["Require eye contact from all", "Seat those students apart", "Lower their participation grade"],
 "Culture shapes communication norms. A teacher who knows this avoids misreading a respectful behavior as inattention."),
("A student listens to a classmate's presentation, then says, \"So your main point is that recycling saves energy. Can you explain the part about aluminum?\" This shows:",
 "Active listening", ["Passive listening", "Interrupting", "Off-task talk"],
 "Restating key points and asking questions are active listening strategies that show attention and build understanding."),
("A teacher asks the question, \"Which planet is closest to the sun?\" This is best described as a:",
 "Convergent question", ["Divergent question", "Evaluative question", "Rhetorical question"],
 "A convergent question has one correct answer. Divergent questions have many possible answers and invite creative thinking."),
("Which question is divergent?",
 "\"How else could we cut litter?\"", ["\"What year did the town begin?\"", "\"Who is the town's founder?\"", "\"Which river runs through it?\""],
 "\"How else could...\" invites many possible answers, so it is divergent. The others have single correct answers."),
("A student is stuck explaining her answer. The teacher says, \"Start with what you did first.\" This is an example of:",
 "Verbal prompting", ["Wait time", "Peer assessment", "Positive punishment"],
 "A verbal prompt gives a cue that helps the student organize and express her ideas without giving the answer."),
("At the end of a lesson a teacher asks, \"What are the three most important ideas we learned today?\" The main purpose of this question is:",
 "Summarizing information", ["Determining prior knowledge", "Motivating a new topic", "Evaluating homework"],
 "End-of-lesson questions that ask students to pull out the key ideas help them summarize and consolidate learning."),
("A teacher's tone rises at the end of \"Great, another mistake.\" The words sound positive but the message is negative. This shows that:",
 "Tone can change the meaning", ["Words matter more than tone", "Students ignore a teacher's tone", "Tone matters only to adults"],
 "Inflection and tone carry meaning. Sarcasm in particular can hurt students and damage trust even when the words seem positive."),
("Which digital tool use best enriches communication in a third-grade class?",
 "A class blog with replies", ["Showing a video with no discussion", "Typing spelling words for points", "Screen time as a quiet reward"],
 "A moderated class blog lets students write for an audience and respond to classmates, extending communication beyond the classroom."),
]
